10. What happens when m1() finishes?

Suppose:

static void m1() {

    int x = 10;

}

When m1() finishes, its stack frame is removed.

Before:

STACK

┌──────────────────┐
│      m1()        │
│      x = 10      │
├──────────────────┤
│      main()      │
└──────────────────┘

After m1() finishes:

STACK

┌──────────────────┐
│      main()      │
└──────────────────┘

The m1() frame no longer exists as an active stack frame.

This is why local variables have method-call lifetime.

11. What if m1() calls another method?

For example:

main()
  ↓
m1()
  ↓
m2()

Stack becomes:

STACK

┌──────────────────┐
│      m2()        │ ← currently executing
├──────────────────┤
│      m1()        │
├──────────────────┤
│      main()      │
└──────────────────┘

If m2() finishes:

STACK

┌──────────────────┐
│      m1()        │ ← continues
├──────────────────┤
│      main()      │
└──────────────────┘

Then m1() finishes:

STACK

┌──────────────────┐
│      main()      │
└──────────────────┘

This is exactly why Stack follows LIFO.

12. Very Important: Method Area vs Stack

This connects directly to your previous doubts.

Suppose:

static void m1(int marks) {

    int x = 50;

}

There are two different things:

Method itself
m1() method code

→ associated with Method Area/class-level runtime information

Execution of m1()
marks = 90
x = 50

→ Stack frame

So:

METHOD AREA
────────────────────
m1() method code
────────────────────
          │
          │ called
          ↓
STACK
────────────────────
m1() frame

marks = 90
x = 50
return information
────────────────────
Remember this sentence:

Method Area stores the method; Stack stores the method's current execution.

13. Instance Method Adds this

Now suppose:

class Student {

    int age = 20;

    void display(int marks) {

        int x = 50;

    }
}

Call:

Student s = new Student();
s.display(90);

Stack:

STACK

display() frame
-------------------------
this ───────→ Student object
marks = 90
x = 50
return information
-------------------------

main() frame
-------------------------
s ──────────→ Student object
-------------------------

Heap:

HEAP

Student object
----------------
age = 20
----------------

So an instance method has the implicit this reference.

A static method doesn't.

